 "observations": [
  "Noise: B vs B2 medians agree within the client resolution (0.05 s) in the fast and thin cells; the mixed cells (2 stalled of 8 peers) are noisy (TTFB J up to 4.9 s) because which blocks land on a stalled peer is random.",
  "Fork regression vs upstream at start (beyond J): fast/4MiB time-to-start B 6.8 s vs A 4.6 s, fast/16MiB 13.2 vs 8.9 s, thin/4MiB 9.6 vs 6.6 s, thin/16MiB 14.5 vs 11.0 s; tail probe at 16 MiB pieces B ~4.2 s vs A 0.05 s. The fork's relay change vs 8c99c7d makes the first and last 3 pieces urgent (6 deadline-0 pieces) where upstream had 1+1, plus tighter window deadlines: the SP02 hypothesis now has measured support (cause attribution still to be shown by GSP02).",
  "Stalled peers dominate the start on both A and B: mixed/4MiB TTFB ~13.5-18 s, mixed/16MiB ~16 s (12-15 s after the first handshake). Neither version recovers blocks requested from a peer that never answers quickly. New candidate SP10 (measure first).",
  "Unused download is large on the fast profile (median ~380-630 MB per run incl. seek), consistent with the G0 finding that dropped deadline pieces stay at priority 1. Not a constraint (UX frame Q2), reported only.",
  "Seek resume is fast where the background download already covered 50 % (fast profile, ~0.05 s) and slow with large pieces on mixed/thin (~6.8-8.2 s).",
  "Tracebacks: 2 of the 10 fork runs (B rep 0, B2 rep 2) in mixed/4MiB logged 2 tracebacks each (app_log counts; raw logs are not kept by design); A had none; failed runs: 0. A diagnostic with masked traceback capture did not reproduce it in 8 short runs; a full-scenario diagnostic follows."
